    "JurisdictionSpecifics": "South Carolina. Murder under S.C. Code § 16-3-10 requires proof beyond a reasonable doubt that the defendant killed another person with malice aforethought. The State's case relies entirely on circumstantial evidence — there is no eyewitness to the shooting, no confession, no murder weapon recovered, and no forensic evidence tying the defendant to the gun. The jury must decide whether the circumstantial evidence, taken together, proves guilt beyond a reasonable doubt.",
