    "Instructions": {
        "Text": "Ladies and gentlemen of the jury, you are instructed as follows:\n\nThe defendant, Marcus Webb, is charged with Murder under South Carolina Code § 16-3-10. To find the defendant guilty of murder, the State must prove beyond a reasonable doubt: (1) the defendant caused the death of Terrence Davis; (2) the defendant acted with malice aforethought; and (3) the killing was unlawful.\n\nMalice aforethought means the defendant acted with a deliberate intention to take a human life, or with a depraved heart and utter disregard for human life.\n\nThis is a case based entirely on CIRCUMSTANTIAL EVIDENCE. Circumstantial evidence is evidence that proves a fact from which you may infer the existence of another fact. It is not direct evidence, such as eyewitness testimony. You may convict based on circumstantial evidence alone if the evidence, taken as a whole, proves guilt beyond a reasonable doubt.\n\nHowever, when the State relies on circumstantial evidence, every reasonable hypothesis other than guilt must be excluded. If the circumstantial evidence is equally consistent with innocence as with guilt, you must find the defendant not guilty.\n\nYou are the sole judges of the credibility of witnesses and the weight to be given to the evidence. You must deliberate together and reach a unanimous verdict. Your verdict must be based solely on the evidence presented and these instructions of law.",
        "Standard": "BeyondReasonableDoubt"
    },
