        {
            "FileName": "Gunshot Residue Analysis - Marcus Webb.pdf",
            "FilePath": "",
            "Summary": "Forensic analysis of swabs taken from Marcus Webb\u0027s hands and clothing approximately 3 hours after the shooting. Gunshot residue (GSR) particles consistent with 9mm ammunition were found on Webb\u0027s RIGHT HAND (12 particles) and the RIGHT SLEEVE of his jacket (8 particles). The GSR analyst testified that these findings are \u0027consistent with\u0027 Webb having fired a weapon, being in close proximity to a discharged firearm, or handling a recently-fired weapon. On CROSS-EXAMINATION, the analyst conceded: (1) GSR can transfer through secondary contact (handshake, touching a contaminated surface); (2) GSR can persist on clothing for 24+ hours; (3) being in a small room when a gun is fired will deposit GSR on everyone present; (4) the number of particles is \u0027on the low end\u0027 for a confirmed shooter. NO GSR was found on Webb\u0027s left hand. The analyst also noted that washing hands significantly reduces GSR — Webb had not washed his hands (suggesting he wasn\u0027t trying to destroy evidence).",
