        {
            "FileName": "Marcus Webb Police Interview - First Statement.pdf",
            "FilePath": "",
            "Summary": "Transcript of Webb\u0027s first police interview, conducted at 2:45 AM at his residence. When asked about his whereabouts that evening, Webb initially stated: \u0027I wasn\u0027t even there that night. I was at my girlfriend\u0027s place all evening.\u0027 When confronted with Russo\u0027s 911 call placing him at the scene, Webb became agitated and asked for a lawyer. PROSECUTION NOTE: This is a provable lie. Webb\u0027s girlfriend later told police Webb left her apartment at approximately 10:30 PM and didn\u0027t return until after midnight. The lie shows consciousness of guilt. DEFENSE NOTE: Webb is a 28-year-old Black man with a prior non-violent drug conviction being questioned by police at 2:45 AM about a murder. Lying to police out of fear is not proof of murder — it\u0027s proof of fear. The Supreme Court has repeatedly recognized that innocent people sometimes lie to police out of fear or panic.",
